# Crop Metadata Template — Image Clipper 3.0

You are describing crops that have ALREADY been cut from one manhwa page. You
are not choosing the crops, not changing their order, and not naming the files.
Those are given to you. Your only job is to write one `description` per crop.

---

# Output contract

**Read this before emitting anything.** Four rules. Each one has broken real
output, and none of them is caught by "does it parse".

## 1. The top level is an OBJECT with exactly one key: `crops`

```json
{
  "crops": [
    { "id": "crop-01", "exportedFilename": "...", "description": "..." }
  ]
}
```

**not** a bare array:

```json
[
  { "id": "crop-01", "exportedFilename": "...", "description": "..." }
]
```

The consumer's check is the first thing it runs, and it is strict:

```js
if (!Array.isArray(parsed?.crops)) → 'Metadata must have a "crops" array.'
```

A bare array has no `.crops`, so `parsed?.crops` is `undefined` and the whole
document is rejected — even when every id, filename and description inside it
is perfect. This is the single most common failure. Wrap the array.

Do not add any other top-level key. No `page`, no `summary`, no `notes`, no
`sourceContext` echoed back. One key: `crops`.

## 2. Emit raw JSON. No fences, no prose, no trailing commentary

The clipboard text is handed to `JSON.parse` verbatim — nothing strips markdown
fences, and nothing tolerates a sentence before or after the document:

```js
const content = await navigator.clipboard.readText()
JSON.parse(content)
```

So the very first character of your reply must be `{` and the very last must
be `}`. A leading ```json line, a closing ```, "Here is the metadata:", or a
follow-up paragraph all make the paste fail with a parse error.

## 3. Reproduce `id` and `exportedFilename` character-for-character

Both come from the EXPORT CONTEXT block, never from you. Copy them exactly:
do not re-number, re-order, re-case, abbreviate, fix a truncated-looking
series slug, or "correct" a reason suffix that reads oddly.

- `id` is matched as a set against the page's own crop points. An id that is
  not in that set, or one that is missing from your output, fails the document.
- `exportedFilename` is matched **positionally** — entry 1 against expected
  filename 1, entry 2 against 2, and so on. So the array order must match the
  EXPORT CONTEXT order exactly. Reordering entries breaks the pairing even when
  every individual name is spelled right.

**Never build a filename out of the crop JSON's `reason` field.** The suffix in
the filename is not the raw reason — it is the reason after slugification, in
which every run of characters outside `a-z0-9` collapses to a single `_`:

```js
reason.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '_').replace(/^_+|_+$/g, '').slice(0, 60)
```

So a crop whose reason is `review:possible_background_only` exports as
`..._07_review_possible_background_only.png` — the colon becomes an
underscore. Copying the reason verbatim produces a name that will never exist
on disk. Most reasons are a plain word like `keep`, where slugification changes
nothing and the trap stays invisible; it only bites on the unusual one. The
EXPORT CONTEXT has already done this conversion for you, which is exactly why
its `exportedFilename` is the only source you may copy from.

The page stem is checked separately and by name. A crop carrying `page_011` on
a `page_007` document is reported as a page mismatch, because such a name
points at a real-but-different page's output and collides with it rather than
simply going missing.

If the EXPORT CONTEXT block is absent, or its `crops` field says the crop JSON
is not attached yet, **stop and say so in plain text**. Do not invent
filenames, do not fall back to the naming pattern, and do not emit a
placeholder slug such as `unknown_series` or an upload UUID.

## 4. One entry per crop, in the given order, and nothing else

Exactly as many entries as `crop_count` in the EXPORT CONTEXT. Every crop
described; no crop described twice; no entry for a crop that is not listed.
Each entry has exactly three keys — `id`, `exportedFilename`, `description` —
in that order. No `reason`, no `label`, no `bbox`, no `needs_review`, no
`notes`.

---

# The `description` field

This is the only field you actually author.

**Shape.** One sentence. Plain prose, no markdown, no line breaks. Roughly
12–35 words. It ends with a period.

**Content.** Describe what is visibly in the crop, in this order of priority:

1. The characters present — how many, and how each is identifiable by
   appearance (hair colour, clothing, build). Include their posture, framing
   (close-up, mid-shot, distant) and expression where readable.
2. The setting or background — indoor/outdoor, terrain, lighting, weather.
3. Significant objects and effects — weapons, energy, magic circles, debris.
4. The presence of speech bubbles or sound effects, noted only as presence
   ("with speech bubbles over the artwork"), never transcribed.

**Identify by appearance, never by name.** Write "a red-haired figure", "a
white-haired character", "a blond figure seated cross-legged". Do not use
character names, and do not infer a name from context or from earlier pages —
these descriptions are read back without the page in view, and a wrong name is
worse than no name.

**Do not transcribe text.** Dialogue, narration boxes, katakana SFX, countdown
numbers and watermarks are not quoted. Note that a bubble or an SFX mark is
present if it is visually prominent, and move on.

**Do not interpret or narrate.** No plot, no motive, no emotion you cannot see,
no continuity between crops. "A red-haired figure stands in profile with
speech bubbles" is right; "the hero finally realises his rival betrayed him"
is not. Each description stands alone — never write "the same character as
above", "continuing from the previous panel", or "meanwhile".

**Describe only what is inside this crop.** Neighbouring panels are not yours
to mention, even when you can see them on the full page.

**Never leave it empty** and never write "unclear", "not visible" or "n/a". A
mostly-abstract crop still has a describable surface: "An ornate golden
blade-like form appears over a dark streaked background with a glowing
countdown timer." If a crop is genuinely blank, say what it is: "A blank pale
panel with no visible artwork."

---

# Worked example

Given an EXPORT CONTEXT whose `crops` field lists:

```json
"crops": [
  { "id": "crop-01", "exportedFilename": "the_sword_saint_reincarnated_into_a_nobl_page_006_01_full_width_tall_scene.png" },
  { "id": "crop-02", "exportedFilename": "the_sword_saint_reincarnated_into_a_nobl_page_006_02_full_width_tall_scene.png" },
  { "id": "crop-03", "exportedFilename": "the_sword_saint_reincarnated_into_a_nobl_page_006_03_full_width_tall_scene.png" }
]
```

emit exactly this — opening brace first, closing brace last, nothing around it:

```json
{
  "crops": [
    {
      "id": "crop-01",
      "exportedFilename": "the_sword_saint_reincarnated_into_a_nobl_page_006_01_full_width_tall_scene.png",
      "description": "Two dark-clad figures stand in a misty outdoor setting, with a red-haired figure in the left foreground and distant figures on the ground behind them."
    },
    {
      "id": "crop-02",
      "exportedFilename": "the_sword_saint_reincarnated_into_a_nobl_page_006_02_full_width_tall_scene.png",
      "description": "A red-haired figure is shown in profile with speech bubbles, while several figures are gathered across an open field around a seated blond figure."
    },
    {
      "id": "crop-03",
      "exportedFilename": "the_sword_saint_reincarnated_into_a_nobl_page_006_03_full_width_tall_scene.png",
      "description": "Several ornate golden blade-like forms appear against a dark streaked background with glowing countdown numbers."
    }
  ]
}
```

